p.src:
The same correspondence holds for products of operators. In $\hat K\hat L$, $\hat L$ acts first and $\hat K$ acts second. Inserting Eq.  between them gives $\begin{aligned}
  (KL)_{nm}
  &=\bra n\hat K\hat L\ket m \\
  &=\sum_r\bra n\hat K\ket r\bra r\hat L\ket m \\
  &=\sum_r K_{nr}L_{rm}.
  \end{aligned}$ This is precisely the rule for multiplying matrices. It also makes clear why operator order matters: in general $\hat K\hat L\neq\hat L\hat K$.

