The first two statements follow directly from Hermiticity. If $\hat H\ket\lambda=\lambda\ket\lambda$, then $\bra\lambda\hat H\ket\lambda
  =\lambda\braket\lambda\lambda.$ The left side is real and the norm is positive, so $\lambda$ is real. For two eigenvectors, $\begin{aligned}
  \bra{\lambda_1}\hat H\ket{\lambda_2}
  &=\lambda_2\braket{\lambda_1}{\lambda_2},\\
  \bra{\lambda_1}\hat H\ket{\lambda_2}
  &=\lambda_1\braket{\lambda_1}{\lambda_2}.\end{aligned}$ Consequently, $(\lambda_2-\lambda_1)\braket{\lambda_1}{\lambda_2}=0.$ Distinct eigenvalues therefore give orthogonal eigenvectors. If an eigenvalue is degenerate, an orthonormal basis can be chosen within its eigenspace. Finally, every eigenvector can be rescaled to unit norm.

