The key calculation is integration by parts. For functions in a common domain with $\phi^*(x)\psi(x)\to0$ at both ends, $\begin{aligned}
  \bra\phi\hat K\ket\psi
  &=
  -i\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
  \phi^*(x)\frac{d\psi}{dx}\,dx\\
  &=
  -i\left[\phi^*(x)\psi(x)\right]_{-\infty}^{\infty}
  +i\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
  \frac{d\phi^*}{dx}\psi(x)\,dx\\
  &=
  \braket{\hat K\phi}{\psi}.
  \end{aligned}$ Thus $\hat K$ is Hermitian on that domain. The boundary conditions are part of the operator; without them, the formal differential expression alone is not enough to establish Hermiticity.

